receipts.text:
{
  "paper": "What Scholars Believe About Jesus' Resurrection (Pt. 1) - Gary Habermas",
  "core_relation": {
    "sentence": "A belief in the resurrection is rationally justified only when a fact is highly evidenced AND a scholarly consensus supports it, with the evidential criterion being by far the more important of the two.",
    "quote": "It's only when accompanied by high it being highly evidence that you can rationally believe. [S385]"
  },
  "product_test": {
    "answer": "additive",
    "quote": "I think number one is so far ahead of number two that I think if number two disappears no biggie. [S403]",
    "reason": "Habermas explicitly states that criterion two (scholarly consensus) can disappear without significant loss, and that criterion one (high evidence) alone is 'sufficient for a rational belief' [S325]. This is additive weighting, not multiplicative collapse: the loss of one factor weakens but does not nullify the conclusion."
  },
  "slots": [
    {
      "slot": "G",
      "plays_role": "External negentropy influx: the outside source of coherence for the argument. In this paper, the external source is the body of published scholarly research (3,700 pages, 8,000 footnotes, multiple attestation, eyewitness and early creedal sources) that sustains the minimal facts against entropy (skeptical objections).",
      "quote": "I've got feet and feet and feet of sources for this thing. 3,700 pages. [S185-S186]",
      "fit": "analogous"
    },
    {
      "slot": "M",
      "plays_role": "Alignment: coupling is strongest when the system is aligned with its reference. Here, the 'reference' is the historical data, and scholars are valued when they align with the data regardless of their theological camp.",
      "quote": "I try to go where the evidence leads and that's my I I hope a lot of us can say that's my credo. [S538]",
      "fit": "analogous"
    },
    {
      "slot": "E",
      "plays_role": "Signal fidelity: whether truth survives transmission through noise. Habermas argues that the minimal facts survive transmission through hostile sources (Bultmann, the Jesus Seminar, atheist scholars) without distortion.",
      "quote": "If you if you know where to look, these people aren't in the book because I generally don't go back to the to 1941 for sources, but you can find these guys and man admits himself way more than that. [S492]",
      "fit": "analogous"
    },
    {
      "slot": "S_eff",
      "plays_role": "Effective entropy: what lowers coherence. In this paper, entropy is the noise of bias, ad hominem attacks, and unfair skepticism that degrades the scholarly consensus.",
      "quote": "It's the guys that are unfair that scream all the time and you go, 'Yeah, well that's what you fundamentalist do.' [S376]",
      "fit": "analogous"
    },
    {
      "slot": "T",
      "plays_role": "Temporal integration: time turns possibility into accumulated consequence. Habermas emphasizes the early dating of creedal statements (within months of the crucifixion) and the long history of research from 1975 onward.",
      "quote": "He says it was probably or may have been or likely have been he says it in different ways out within months. [S251]",
      "fit": "direct"
    },
    {
      "slot": "K",
      "plays_role": "Compression: ordered meaning compresses and noise does not. The minimal facts themselves are a compression of the vast evidence into six compact, highly attested propositions.",
      "quote": "I can find my six facts over and over and over and over and over. [S439]",
      "fit": "direct"
    },
    {
      "slot": "R",
      "plays_role": "Phase transition: some thresholds change state, not just degree. Habermas describes a threshold of evidence beyond which belief becomes rational, though he refuses to name a percentage.",
      "quote": "I do not have a certain threshold that they have to have to meet. [S206]",
      "fit": "stretched"
    },
    {
      "slot": "Q",
      "plays_role": "Superposition: open possibility stays unresolved until actualized. The paper treats the resurrection as a historical event that was once an open question but is now settled by evidence and consensus.",
      "quote": "What about the resurrection? Is it not a myth? Pure and simple. [S504]",
      "fit": "stretched"
    },
    {
      "slot": "F",
      "plays_role": "Non-local correlation: related systems are no longer independent. Habermas argues that scholars across ideological spectra (from Bultmann to evangelical) converge on the minimal facts, showing non-local correlation.",
      "quote": "when guys who are not even in my camp, and he's not the only one will compliment something, um, I'm happy for it. [S371]",
      "fit": "analogous"
    },
    {
      "slot": "C",
      "plays_role": "Integration: the local integrator inside the product. Habermas himself is the integrator, synthesizing 3,700 pages of research into a coherent argument.",
      "quote": "I'm the author of these four volumes. I'm setting down my own criteria. [S659-S660]",
      "fit": "direct"
    }
  ],
  "breaks": [
    "The product test is explicitly additive, not multiplicative: Habermas says if criterion two disappears 'no biggie' [S403], which contradicts the zero-collapse property of χ.",
    "Slot R (phase transition) is stretched: Habermas refuses to define a threshold, saying 'I do not have a certain threshold' [S206], so there is no clear phase transition.",
    "Slot Q (superposition) is stretched: the resurrection is treated as a settled historical fact, not an open possibility.",
    "The paper adds a factor with no slot: the 'silly skeptical softball objection' and the meta-discussion of how skeptics criticize Habermas for not naming names—this is about the sociology of the debate, not the master equation factors."
  ],
  "extra_factors": [
    "The role of the publisher and editor (Ben Shaw) in shaping the volumes—this is a social/institutional factor not captured by the 10 slots.",
    "The 'silly skeptical softball objection' about naming names—a rhetorical and social dynamic outside the χ framework."
  ],
  "analog_strength": 2,
  "confidence": "medium",
  "one_line": "The paper's core relation is an additive two-criterion test for rational belief in the resurrection, where the evidential criterion dominates and the consensus criterion is optional, making the χ analogy weak because it lacks multiplicative collapse.",
  "equations_present": false
}
